---

<Layout title = "Elementary motions" description = "placeholder.">

  <div slot="navtree">
    <ul class='list-group list-group-flush py-0'> 
      <li class = 'list-group-item py-0'><a class = 'text-decoration-none subsection' href = '#rke-sr'>Rectilinear motion</a>
      <li class = 'list-group-item py-0'><a class = 'text-decoration-none subsection' href = '#rke-sc'>Circular motion</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <Section title = "Elementary motions" id = "rke">
    <p>
      Two of the most common motions are straight-line and
      circular kinematics. While the general kinematic formulas
      apply to these situations, we can write simpler formulas
      just for these special cases.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <SubSection title="Rectilinear motion" id = "rke-sr">
    <p>
      Rectilinear motion is motion in a straight line. In the
      particular case that acceleration is constant, the velocity
      and position can be found by explicit integration.
    </p>
    <DisplayEquation equation = `\\begin{align} a &= a_0 = \\text{constant} \\\\
      v &= v_0 + a_0 t \\\\
      x &= x_0 + v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2} a_0 t ^ 2 \\end{align}` title = "Constant linear acceleration." background = "True" id = "rke-er"/>
    <p>
      If the <InlineEquation equation = "x"/> and <InlineEquation equation = "y"/> components of a 2D motion are
      independent, then each direction can be regarded as simple
      rectilinear motion. For example, if a projectile flies
      through the air then the horizontal component is linear in
      <InlineEquation equation = "t"/> while the vertical motion with constant gravitational
      acceleration is a parabola.
    </p>
  </SubSection>

  <SubSection title = "Circular motion" id = "rke-sc">
    <p>
      Motion in a circle of constant radius gives velocity in the
      tangential direction <InlineEquation equation = "\\hat{e}_{\\theta}."/> The acceleration has
      one component directly inwards in the radial direction
      (centripetal acceleration) and one tangential component due
      to angular acceleration <InlineEquation equation = "\\alpha."/>
    </p>
    <DisplayEquation equation = `\\begin{align} r &= \\text{constant} \\\\
      \\vec{v} &= r \\omega \\, \\hat{e}_{\\theta} \\\\
      \\vec{a} &= - r \\omega ^ 2 \\, \\hat{e}_r + r \\alpha \\, \\hat{e}_{\\theta} \\end{align}` title = "Circular motion (constant \\(r\\))." background = "True" id = "rke-ec"/>
    <p>
      Note that for circular motion the velocity <InlineEquation equation = "v"/> is linear in
      both the radius <InlineEquation equation = "r"/> and angular velocity <InlineEquation equation = "\\omega."/> The
      tangential acceleration is linear in both the radius <InlineEquation equation = "r"/> and
      angular acceleration <InlineEquation equation = "\\alpha,"/> while the radial acceleration
      is linear in <InlineEquation equation = "r"/> but quadratic in <InlineEquation equation = "\\omega."/>
    </p>
    <p>
      In the case of constant angular acceleration, the angular
      components function like rectilinear motion but in a circle,
      giving the explicit formulas:
    </p>
    <DisplayEquation equation = `\\begin{align} r &= r_0 = \\text{constant} \\\\
      \\alpha &= \\alpha_0 = \\text{constant} \\\\
      \\omega &= \\omega_0 + \\alpha_0 t \\\\
      \\theta &= \\theta_0 + \\omega_0 t + \\frac{1}{2} \\alpha_0 t ^ 2 \\end{align}` title = "Constant angular acceleration." background = "True" id = "rke-ea"/>
  </SubSection>
  
</Layout>

<script src="/dyn/particle_kinematics/canvases.js" is:inline></script>
